<p>Decimal 36866. Default value: 150. Also known as <strong>VICCR2</strong>.</p>
<p>This register has two functions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bit 7</strong> - bit 9 of the video RAM address. On an unexpanded VIC 20 the
screen sits at $1E00 so this bit is set; when the screen moves to $1000 (8K or
more expansion) the bit is clear.</li>
<li><strong>Bits 6-0</strong> - the number of character columns the chip draws. Normally 22.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because bit 7 is shared, always keep it when writing a new column count:</p>
<code>
// draw 16 columns instead of 22, leaving the screen address alone
SCREEN_TEXT_WIDTH := (SCREEN_TEXT_WIDTH &amp; 128) + 16;
</code>

<p>Bit 7 also decides where colour RAM starts: set means colour memory begins at
38400 ($9600), clear means 37888 ($9400). In BASIC that is:</p>
<code>
C = 37888 + 4 * ( PEEK ( 36866 ) AND 128 )
</code>
<p>In TRSE just use SCREEN_COL_LOC, which already holds the right address for the
memory configuration the project is built for.</p>
